<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>Harness.S7</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Harness.S7</AssemblyName>

    <!--
      Where Sharp7 comes from. SETTLED 2026-09-20 (owner's decision): a NuGet PackageReference,
      pinned to 1.1.82 in Directory.Packages.props, which is where the reasoning and the 🔴 caveat
      live. This replaced a $(Sharp7Path) property pointing at a machine-local DLL that was never
      committed — which is why that path, its Exists() conditionals and the warn target are gone.
      Read the note in Directory.Packages.props before changing the version: the swap changed the
      binary, so no existing rig measurement transfers.
    -->
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The composition root, and the only project that references both halves. Harness knows nothing
      of DeviceGuard and DeviceGuard knows nothing of Harness — that separation is deliberate and is
      what makes both testable alone, so this is the single place the two are allowed to meet.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Harness\Harness.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\device-guard\DeviceGuard\DeviceGuard.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Unconditional now, and the graceful degradation that used to live here is deliberately gone.
    Sharp7Client.cs was excluded from the build whenever the machine-local DLL was absent, so that
    the fence wiring and the fake-client tests could still run anywhere. That existed to stop a
    machine-local path becoming a hard build dependency WHILE THE VENDORING QUESTION WAS OPEN. It is
    closed, the package restores on any machine, and a conditional compile that can silently drop
    the real device adapter is worse than a build error once there is nothing left for it to absorb.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Sharp7" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
